WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.920
INTRO:
Mac vs PC is one of the oldest rivalries within&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:03.920 --> 00:00:09.440
the tech industry. Windows has dominated this race&nbsp;
in terms of market share and will likely continue&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.440 --> 00:00:15.360
to do so moving forward, but I think Apple’s share&nbsp;
of this market is about to grow substantially over&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.360 --> 00:00:21.040
the next decade. Now, many Apple fanboys will&nbsp;
claim that it’s about time given that Macs were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.040 --> 00:00:27.440
always better than PCs, but I don’t think that’s&nbsp;
exactly true. We don’t have to look that far back&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.440 --> 00:00:33.040
to see how limiting Macs were in the past. For&nbsp;
example, remember how Macbooks only had USB-C&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.040 --> 00:00:38.560
ports or how Apple shipped unreliable butterfly&nbsp;
keyboards just to make the keyboard mm thinner&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.560 --> 00:00:44.960
or the time that Apple recalled 432,000 MacBook&nbsp;
Pros because they would catch on fire? Clearly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.960 --> 00:00:50.240
Macs haven’t always been perfect, but ever since&nbsp;
Apple ditched Intel and released their M1 series&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.240 --> 00:00:56.080
of products, I think Apple’s offerings have become&nbsp;
more appealing than ever before. And I’m not the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.080 --> 00:01:01.920
only one who thinks this either as their annual&nbsp;
growth has far outpaced the PC market. Last year,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.920 --> 00:01:08.000
annual sales for PCs grew by 14.6% while&nbsp;
the annual sales for the Mac grew by 28.3%&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.560 --> 00:01:13.840
or nearly 2 times as much. Also, this&nbsp;
growth wasn’t just amongst rich Americans,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.840 --> 00:01:19.760
the laptop market, or liberal college students,&nbsp;
this growth was within the global PC market.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.760 --> 00:01:25.040
Considering this, I don’t think you’d be surprised&nbsp;
to hear that Apple hit record Mac revenue numbers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.040 --> 00:01:30.480
pulling in over $10 billion within a single&nbsp;
quarter. So, here’s why Mac adoption is going&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.480 --> 00:01:39.840
through the roof and why Mac’s market share&nbsp;
may grow as high as the iPhone’s market share.

00:01:39.840 --> 00:01:42.480
VALUE:
Likely one of the biggest factors driving&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.480 --> 00:01:48.160
up Mac adoption recently is that Apple has become&nbsp;
more competitive than ever before when it comes to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.160 --> 00:01:54.560
value. Now, this may be a bit confusing as we see&nbsp;
Apple continuously churn out extremely expensive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.560 --> 00:02:02.640
products. After all, they do sell a $50,000 PC and&nbsp;
a $5000 monitor, but how many people do you really&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.640 --> 00:02:08.400
think buy these products. The top-end Mac Pro is&nbsp;
literally equivalent to the average American’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.400 --> 00:02:13.280
annual salary. And while you may think that&nbsp;
ripping off customers is Apple’s entire business&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.280 --> 00:02:19.360
plan, if you look deeper into these products,&nbsp;
you’ll know that isn’t 100% true given that Apple&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.360 --> 00:02:24.480
doesn’t even take these products seriously. One of&nbsp;
my favorite tests to do when it comes to expensive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.480 --> 00:02:31.280
Apple products is to see how long it takes them&nbsp;
to ship to me. Today is March 27, 2022, and if I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.280 --> 00:02:37.280
order the Apple Pro Stand right now, it’ll take&nbsp;
until April 8th for it to arrive or 12 days.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.280 --> 00:02:42.560
Meanwhile, if I order the Mac mini, it’ll arrive&nbsp;
in just 2 days or I could pick it up in-store&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.560 --> 00:02:48.720
today. Now, if Apple was really trying to sell the&nbsp;
Pro Stand seriously, it wouldn’t take them 12 days&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.720 --> 00:02:54.480
to deliver it. I’m literally spending $1000 on a&nbsp;
monitor stand, you would think they would at least&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.480 --> 00:03:00.640
spend some effort in fast delivery. But they don’t&nbsp;
because they don’t take the Pro Stand seriously.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.640 --> 00:03:05.200
All these products that people love to make fun of&nbsp;
like the Pro Stand, and Apple Wheels, and the Mac&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:05.200 --> 00:03:11.040
Pro are all headline products. While a very small&nbsp;
number of people will actually buy these products&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.040 --> 00:03:16.000
and may even find them valuable, the primary&nbsp;
purpose of these products is not to generate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.000 --> 00:03:21.200
sales but to generate headlines like this. Such&nbsp;
headlines serve 2 purposes which are building&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.200 --> 00:03:27.120
exclusivity and price anchoring. The first purpose&nbsp;
needs no explanation. When consumers constantly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:27.120 --> 00:03:32.800
see such outrageous products, they start to&nbsp;
associate Apple with exclusivity and desirability.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.800 --> 00:03:37.760
And, in terms of price anchoring, when people&nbsp;
see a $50,000 computer, they’ll start to think&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.760 --> 00:03:44.000
that a $2 or $3000 computer is relatively not that&nbsp;
expensive. If we put such products aside though,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.000 --> 00:03:49.600
we’ll see that Apple is extremely competitive in&nbsp;
terms of price and value. For example, in terms&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.600 --> 00:03:56.160
of desktops, the M1 Mac Mini retails for $700&nbsp;
and you can actually get it for $669 at Amazon.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.800 --> 00:04:01.040
Gary from the Everyday Dad, for example, thinks&nbsp;
that the Mac Mini is the best value computer you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:01.040 --> 00:04:07.760
can buy on the market, and I think that’s a&nbsp;
pretty accurate description of this machine.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:07.760 --> 00:04:12.560
If you disagree, I challenge you to comment down&nbsp;
below a desktop computer that’s more portable,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:12.560 --> 00:04:17.040
easy to use and offers more performance&nbsp;
for the same price as the M1 Mac Mini.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:17.040 --> 00:04:22.320
If we look at Apple’s Macbook lineup, it’s exactly&nbsp;
the same case there as well. While the MacBook&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:22.320 --> 00:04:27.760
pros aren’t the easiest to justify for the average&nbsp;
person, just take a look at the MacBook Air. It&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:27.760 --> 00:04:33.360
starts at $1000 which makes it the same price&nbsp;
as an iPhone 13 pro. And if you’re a student,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:33.360 --> 00:04:39.360
you can actually get it for $900. Now again,&nbsp;
you can find plenty of laptops for $300 to $500.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:40.160 --> 00:04:43.360
But, when you compare the MacBook Air to&nbsp;
other laptops that are priced at $1000,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:44.400 --> 00:04:49.920
it’s extremely difficult if not impossible to&nbsp;
find a laptop that offers more value per dollar.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:49.920 --> 00:04:53.520
So, while Apple still doesn’t sell&nbsp;
the super cheap entry-level products,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:53.520 --> 00:04:58.400
the entry-level products that Apple does sell are&nbsp;
basically impossible to beat thanks to the M1.

00:05:01.560 --> 00:05:02.560
SYNERGY:&nbsp;

00:05:02.560 --> 00:05:08.320
Moving on, the second reason that Macs have been&nbsp;
doing better than ever before is their synergy.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:08.320 --> 00:05:13.040
There’s of course the Apple ecosystem which is&nbsp;
getting harder and harder to escape by the day,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:13.040 --> 00:05:19.040
but I’m not even talking about that. Rather, I’m&nbsp;
talking about hardware synergy. One of the top&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:19.040 --> 00:05:23.440
reasons for the success of the iPhone is that&nbsp;
Apple is able to leverage so much more power&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:23.440 --> 00:05:28.480
out of the same hardware. iPhones consistently&nbsp;
tend to have less ram, less computing power,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:28.480 --> 00:05:34.480
and less graphics power than Android counterparts.&nbsp;
Despite this, in terms of real-world performance,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:34.480 --> 00:05:38.640
iPhones often match these Android counterparts&nbsp;
if not beat them thanks to their hardware&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:38.640 --> 00:05:43.680
and software optimization. The lack of such&nbsp;
optimization was one of the biggest shortfalls&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:43.680 --> 00:05:49.360
of the Mac, but that’s no longer the case. Of&nbsp;
course, there’s the M1, but that’s not the only&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:49.360 --> 00:05:54.720
factor. Take Apple’s unified memory, for example.&nbsp;
Several reports and reviews have suggested that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:54.720 --> 00:05:59.600
Macs with unified memory not only match but&nbsp;
outperform systems with twice as much RAM.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:00.240 --> 00:06:05.680
Also, it’s not just Apple that’s leveraging this&nbsp;
optimized hardware. Some of the biggest software&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:05.680 --> 00:06:11.280
developers are finally taking the time to optimize&nbsp;
their software for Mac. Adobe, for example, claims&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:11.280 --> 00:06:17.680
that their apps are now 80% faster on M1 Macs. And&nbsp;
looking forward, this synergy should only improve&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:17.680 --> 00:06:22.880
as Apple bridges the gap between their iOS&nbsp;
devices and their Mac devices. Historically,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:22.880 --> 00:06:28.000
developers have not wanted to develop software for&nbsp;
Mac because it’s always been such a niche market.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:28.000 --> 00:06:32.960
Something that’s not a niche market though is the&nbsp;
iOS app store. And with the introduction of Apple&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:32.960 --> 00:06:38.880
silicon on Macs, software development for iOS and&nbsp;
macOS has basically become identical. According&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:38.880 --> 00:06:43.200
to Apple themselves, “Most iOS apps require&nbsp;
no modifications to run on Apple silicon.”&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:43.760 --> 00:06:48.160
Now, this doesn’t mean that Supercell is&nbsp;
going to develop Clash Royale for the Mac. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:48.160 --> 00:06:52.400
what this does mean though is that software&nbsp;
companies who primarily deal with Windows&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:52.400 --> 00:06:57.200
can leverage the large base of iOS developers&nbsp;
to efficiently port over software to Mac.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:57.200 --> 00:07:02.080
So, in the coming years, Apple will not only have&nbsp;
some of the most value-packed hardware but some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:02.080 --> 00:07:06.960
of the most optimized software. And given that&nbsp;
this combination is what allowed them to perform&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:06.960 --> 00:07:15.840
so well within the mobile market, I’d bet it’ll&nbsp;
play out pretty well within the PC market as well.

00:07:15.840 --> 00:07:17.600
AREAS OF IMPROVEMENT:
While Apple’s synergy and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:17.600 --> 00:07:22.320
value are great if you choose the right products,&nbsp;
we can’t ignore that Macs still have a few major&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:22.320 --> 00:07:27.280
pitfalls starting with a lack of repairability&nbsp;
and upgradability. At the end of last year,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:27.280 --> 00:07:31.600
Apple announced their self-service repair program&nbsp;
which promised to allow consumers to repair their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:31.600 --> 00:07:37.440
own iPhones and Macs with genuine Apple parts.&nbsp;
But, with the recent release of the Mac Studio,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:37.440 --> 00:07:41.840
it’s not clear how true Apple will stay to&nbsp;
this promise. If you guys haven’t heard yet,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:41.840 --> 00:07:46.400
if you crack open the Mac Studio, you’ll actually&nbsp;
find that the storage device is removable and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:46.400 --> 00:07:51.680
replaceable. However, what Luke Miani found was&nbsp;
that if you switch the slot in which the storage&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:51.680 --> 00:07:56.560
device is placed or swap the storage device with&nbsp;
another storage device with a different capacity,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:56.560 --> 00:08:01.440
the Mac Studio refuses to boot. Clearly,&nbsp;
there’s some sort of software lock and/or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:01.440 --> 00:08:07.040
encryption that prevents users from changing the&nbsp;
storage which is quite stupid. I have no idea why&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:07.040 --> 00:08:12.480
Apple doesn’t just let people swap the storage&nbsp;
because here’s the thing. I bet 95% of Apple’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:12.480 --> 00:08:17.760
customers won’t want to take apart a brand new&nbsp;
computer and try to upgrade the storage. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:17.760 --> 00:08:22.400
even if Apple offered this option, these guys&nbsp;
would just pay Apple for the extra storage&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:22.400 --> 00:08:28.320
anyway, so it’s not like Apple would lose out&nbsp;
on much revenue. In the meantime, they can avoid&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:28.320 --> 00:08:32.720
all the criticism they receive regarding lack&nbsp;
of repairability. The second biggest shortfall&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:32.720 --> 00:08:36.800
of Macs is sort of related to the first&nbsp;
point which is their base storage options.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:36.800 --> 00:08:41.600
While the Mac Mini and MacBook air offer great&nbsp;
value in terms of performance, they don’t offer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:41.600 --> 00:08:47.680
great value in terms of storage. These machines&nbsp;
should offer a minimum of 512 GB of storage,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:47.680 --> 00:08:53.200
and their higher-end Mac Studios and MacBook Pros&nbsp;
should offer a minimum of 1 TB of storage. Their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:53.200 --> 00:08:59.280
current storage sizes are unacceptable in 2022.&nbsp;
Aside from lack of repairability and underwhelming&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:59.280 --> 00:09:03.600
starting storage, the last biggest complaint&nbsp;
regarding Macs is their limited software.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:03.600 --> 00:09:08.240
If you want to play games, for example, there’s&nbsp;
still no reason to get a mac and I don’t think&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:08.240 --> 00:09:13.360
this will change anytime soon. Similarly, some&nbsp;
high-end software like the Autodesk suite have yet&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:13.360 --> 00:09:18.400
to really be refined for Apple silicon. However,&nbsp;
unlike gaming, I think this can improve in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:18.400 --> 00:09:23.120
imminent future as software companies leverage iOS&nbsp;
developers, but we’ll just have to wait and see.

00:09:23.120 --> 00:09:27.200
FUTURE OF MAC:&nbsp;

00:09:27.200 --> 00:09:31.520
While Macs do still have some shortfalls, I&nbsp;
don’t think these shortfalls will actually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:31.520 --> 00:09:36.400
sway the average consumer that much. Like&nbsp;
I said, the average consumer won’t want to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:36.400 --> 00:09:41.200
open up their computer anyway, so a&nbsp;
lack of repairability/upgradeability&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:41.200 --> 00:09:44.880
isn’t that big of a deal, though it&nbsp;
would be nice if Apple allowed for this.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:45.440 --> 00:09:50.080
And in terms of software limitations, most people&nbsp;
will mainly just spend their time doing work and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:50.080 --> 00:09:54.800
browsing through social media. And unless&nbsp;
you’re an architect or 3D designer or something,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:54.800 --> 00:09:59.200
the software limitations wouldn’t even apply to&nbsp;
you. Something else to note is that all of these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:59.200 --> 00:10:04.480
issues are relatively easy to fix. It’s not that&nbsp;
hard for Apple to bump up the starting storage&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:04.480 --> 00:10:09.360
or lighten up on the software restrictions that&nbsp;
prevent upgrades. Recently, Apple has been a lot&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:09.360 --> 00:10:13.840
more open to feedback given that they brought&nbsp;
back SD card slots, HDMI ports, and regular&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:13.840 --> 00:10:18.240
USB ports on some of their newer machines. So&nbsp;
hopefully, they’ll make these changes as well.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:18.240 --> 00:10:22.720
If they do make these changes, I think Mac&nbsp;
adoption will only accelerate moving forward,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:22.720 --> 00:10:27.920
especially with the launch of the M2 chip. Now,&nbsp;
I don’t think Mac will ever become the dominant&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:27.920 --> 00:10:33.440
PC operating system just like iOS will never&nbsp;
become the dominant mobile operating system.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:33.440 --> 00:10:38.640
However, I do think Apple can grow their PC&nbsp;
market share to closer their mobile market share.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:38.640 --> 00:10:44.960
Right now, Macs only account for about 10% of the&nbsp;
global PC market while iOS accounts for 28% of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:44.960 --> 00:10:49.760
global mobile market. Due to all the reasons&nbsp;
outlined so far in this video, I think it’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:49.760 --> 00:10:54.800
quite possible that over the next decade, Apple’s&nbsp;
market share in the PC market grows closer to 20%.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:55.680 --> 00:10:59.840
This is quite odd for me to say actually cause&nbsp;
I’ve always been a massive fan of Windows due to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:59.840 --> 00:11:04.560
its versatility and customizability. The first&nbsp;
videos on this YouTube channel are actually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:04.560 --> 00:11:09.440
videos of me building my PC and discussing&nbsp;
the GPU market. It wasn’t till recently that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:09.440 --> 00:11:14.160
I got my first Mac, and while I now have&nbsp;
the option of using either Windows or Mac,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:14.160 --> 00:11:18.640
I honestly don’t think I’ll be using my Windows&nbsp;
computer that much moving forward because the Mac&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:18.640 --> 00:11:24.800
can do everything I need but better, faster, and&nbsp;
smoother. And as more people give the M1 a shot,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:24.800 --> 00:11:29.440
I think they’ll come to the same conclusion,&nbsp;
but that’s just what I think. Are you guys team&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:29.440 --> 00:11:34.560
Windows or team Mac? Comment that down below.&nbsp;
Also, drop a like if you think Apple killed it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:34.560 --> 00:11:39.040
with the M1. And of course, consider checking out&nbsp;
our international channels to watch our videos in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:39.040 --> 00:11:42.720
other languages and consider subscribing&nbsp;
